<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antoni Radziwiłł</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Antoni Henryk Radziwiłł</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Antoni Radziwiłł was a Polish-Lithuanian aristocrat, composer and patron of the arts who married Princess Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. From 1815 to 1831 he served as the Prussian king's governor of the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a>, mediating between the Polish nobility and the Prussian state. A friend of Goethe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Chopin</a>, he composed music for Goethe's Faust.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoni-radziwill.jpg" alt="Antoni Radziwiłł" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Oldermann / After Franz Krüger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Oldermann_nach_Franz_Kr%C3%BCger_Antoni_Henryk_Radziwi%C5%82%C5%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antoni Henryk Radziwiłł was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> on 13 June 1775, the son of Michał Hieronim Radziwiłł and Helena Przeździecka, and studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> with his brothers. As a scion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radziwill-family">Radziwiłł family</a> he was hereditary Duke of Nieśwież and Ołyka and a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The Hohenzollern court offered him the hand of Princess Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>; the marriage took place in 1796, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> had ended the Polish state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His new family persuaded him to act as a go-between for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> of the Prussian partition and the authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Moving between Berlin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, he proposed making South Prussia the core of a renewed Polish kingdom under the Prussian king in personal union. In 1806 he tried and failed to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Prince Józef Poniatowski</a> away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>; the Greater Poland Uprising instead led to the Duchy of Warsaw.</p><p>After the Congress of Vienna King Frederick William III sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> as Duke-Governor of the Grand Duchy of Posen. He had little real power, which lay with the Oberpräsident Joseph Zerboni di Sposetti and the district governors, and in 1824 his daughter Eliza's engagement to Prince William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the future German Emperor William I, was broken off. When his brother Michał Gedeon Radziwiłł led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830 in Congress Poland, he was stripped of his powers, and the office was abolished the next year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Radziwiłł is remembered more as a patron of the arts. In his palaces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> and Antonin he himself performed guitar, cello and opera concerts, and his guests included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-paganini">Niccolò Paganini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Frédéric Chopin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a>. Chopin wrote his Introduction and Polonaise Op. 3 for him and dedicated his Piano Trio Op. 8 to him, and Beethoven dedicated the overture Zur Namensfeier, Op. 115. He died in Berlin on 7 April 1833 and was buried in Poznań Cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antoni Radziwiłł” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The king of Prussia ruled through a Polish governor, Prince <b>Antoni Radziwill</b>, and the Polish language was used in schools and courts.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-posen">Grand Duchy of Posen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.69,25.28&amp;year=1812" aria-label="Vilnius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Antoni Radziwiłł"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4767 5445h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4767 5445h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.69,25.28&amp;year=1812">What was here in 1812? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
